    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense but it still feels like there's some kind of hidden drag on the payments. like, why does it feel like the interest is intentionally trying to keep the balance high? is there anything specifically that causes that \"slow\" feeling in the beginning?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're not imagining it—that \"drag\" is the mathematical reality of compound interest working against you. In the early years, your balance is at its largest, so the daily interest accrual is also at its peak, meaning a bigger slice of each payment is consumed by interest before any principal is touched. This is especially pronounced with longer terms (like 10–20 years) or higher rates, because the lender structures the payment to keep you paying mostly interest upfront. It's not intentional malice, but it *is* designed to maximize their profit over the loan's life—so the \"slow\" feeling is baked into the amortization schedule. To speed it up, you'd need to make extra principal-only payments, which directly reduce the balance and thus the future interest drag.",
      "source": "target",
